The backend runs on a physical machine inside the home LAN rather than on the cloud VPS used for other projects, so nothing needs to be exposed externally - but HTTPS inside a LAN is exactly what old Safari is worst at (mixed content, self-signed certificates), which makes the reverse-proxy/TLS question a deployment-phase risk rather than a settled one.
