[node:a17b142e-850e-4cdb-8d84-2689e75428a7]
type: decision
tier: lifetime
path: /artifact/work-reaches-main-one-branch-at-a-time
created_at: 2026-09-08T09:55:55.888852+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> CONTRADICTS [node:8a963777-1d94-42ad-ae4b-8a2b08d0a686] @ 2026-09-08T09:55:55.888852+00:00
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:8a963777-1d94-42ad-ae4b-8a2b08d0a686] @ 2026-09-08T10:09:26.383970+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:c62c070b-e4a6-41b3-86f3-00ad5d96800f] @ 2026-09-08T09:55:55.888852+00:00

Work reaches main one branch at a time, through a pull request, and lands as a merge commit: squash merging and rebase merging are both turned off. A change is a sequence of phases with a memory capture at each boundary, so the per-phase commits are the readable record of that sequence - squashing discards it and leaves one commit whose message cannot honestly describe what the phase boundaries decided, and rebasing rewrites hashes that captured nodes and review comments already point at. Branches carry the same id as the change folder and the memory scope, so branch, folder and graph agree by construction.
---
